# Sending files to the user (attachments)

This guide applies to chat workspace `{{.ChatFolder}}`. A path in your reply is
NOT an attachment: the user only gets a file when the host accepts an IPC
attachment request. You do have this tool; do not tell the user you cannot send
files.

## How to attach a file

1. Write the file under `/workspace/chat` (for example
   `/workspace/chat/banks/report.pdf`). Regular files only: no symlinks, no
   hard links, no hidden (`.name`) or `memory/` paths, not empty.
2. Publish one request atomically: write `<name>.tmp`, then rename it to
   `<name>.json` in `/workspace/ipc/messages/`. Use a new unique `<name>` per
   request and the same value as `requestId` (8-64 characters: letters, digits,
   `-`, `_`; starting with a letter or digit).

```bash
name="attach-$(date +%s)-$RANDOM"
cat > "/workspace/ipc/messages/$name.tmp" <<JSON
{"version":1,"requestId":"$name","capability":"$GOPOD_ARTIFACT_CAP",
 "files":[{"path":"/workspace/chat/banks/report.pdf","caption":"Bank assessment","as":"auto"}]}
JSON
mv "/workspace/ipc/messages/$name.tmp" "/workspace/ipc/messages/$name.json"
```

3. Wait for the receipt `/workspace/ipc/results/messages/<name>.result.json`
   (usually a few seconds) and read it before you answer:

```bash
for i in $(seq 1 30); do [ -f "/workspace/ipc/results/messages/$name.result.json" ] && break; sleep 1; done
cat "/workspace/ipc/results/messages/$name.result.json"
```

## Request fields

- `version` must be `1`; `capability` is `$GOPOD_ARTIFACT_CAP` from your
  environment, verbatim. Unknown fields reject the whole request.
- `files`: 1 to 4 entries. Per file:
  - `path` (required): absolute, clean, under `/workspace/chat/`.
  - `caption` (optional): plain text, at most 1024 UTF-16 units.
  - `filename` (optional): the name the user sees; default is the file's own name.
  - `as` (optional): `auto` (default), `document` or `photo`.
    `auto` sends PNG/JPEG as a photo and everything else (PDF, spreadsheets,
    archives, text, other images) as a document with its filename.
    `document` always sends the original file. `photo` is allowed only for
    PNG/JPEG; a photo Telegram cannot take falls back to a document.
- `text` (optional): a message sent before the files. Usually leave it out and
  answer in your normal reply instead.
- JSON-escape quotes and backslashes in `caption`, or leave the caption out.

## Limits

20 MiB per file and 40 MiB per request (below Telegram's 50 MB bot limit), plus
per-run and hourly host quotas. Attachments work in the owner's private chat
only; elsewhere every request is refused with `artifacts_disabled`.

## Reading the receipt

- `status` `queued` or `sent`: accepted; the host delivers the file. In your
  reply say briefly that you sent it; do not paste the path again.
- `status` `uncertain`: it may have arrived; never resend.
- `status` `error`: NOT attached. `error` is the reason, for example
  `artifacts_disabled` (sending files is off or this run has no capability),
  `path_outside_workspace`, `path_denied`, `file_too_large`, `unsupported_type`
  (`as:"photo"` on a non-PNG/JPEG), `source_missing`, `empty_file`,
  `not_regular_file`, `hardlink_denied`, `stale_capability`, `quota_exceeded`,
  `rate_limited`, `storage_full`, `invalid_request`.
  The host also tells the user which file was not attached. In your reply say
  that the file is at its path and could not be attached, with the reason.
  Never claim a refused file was sent. Retry only after fixing the cause, with a
  new name and requestId.
